+### Edge schedule
+
+The base win chance and the $FLIPPER payout slide with the size of the treasury (`setEdgeSchedule`, owner):
+
+| treasury (× the launch treasury, `refTreasury` = 150M) | base win chance | $FLIPPER payout | $FLIPPER edge | token edge (free route) |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| below 2× | 45% | 2.05× | 7.75% | 10% |
+| 4× | 46.25% | 2.025× | 6.34% | 7.5% |
+| 6× and above | 47.5% | 2.0× | 5% | 5% |
+
+Linear in between, and back again as the treasury shrinks: the house is deliberately more protective in a drawdown.
+A flip keeps the odds and payout it was made at. The schedule sets only the base: route costs (the chance-based
+fee), the minimum edge (`minHouseEdgeBps`, 2%, enforced on every flip, also between the schedule's endpoints) and
+partner discounts still apply on top. The owner sets every value within bounds: win chances within
+[`minWinChanceBps`, 49%], payouts within [2×, 2.15×], the edge at both ends at or above `minHouseEdgeBps`, and
+from < to. `refTreasury` 0 turns it off (then Params' `baseWinChanceBps` / `flipperPayoutBps` apply). Views:
+`currentBaseWinChanceBps()`, `currentFlipperPayoutBps()`, `edgeSchedule()`.
+
+The metric is the house's own `treasury` in $FLIPPER, not a USD value: it reads no price, pool or oracle, so a flash
+loan or a pool manipulation can't move the odds. An inflow can: a donation is a gift to the bankroll that buys
+far less expected value than it costs (at most 2.75% of each capped stake), and a vault deposit is locked for 30 days
+while every flip stays negative for the player (at least a 5% edge at the schedule's end, 2% after route costs).
+
